WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.890
- What is the most ethical career path

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:04.500
that you can choose?

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.210
Can it be possible that a career

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.020
in consulting or banking

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:12.060
is more ethical than a
career as a medical doctor?

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.030
So these are the question

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:15.760
that I'd like investigate with you today.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.090
To clearly say this in the beginning,

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:18.059
of course, I am aware

00:00:18.059 --> 00:00:19.660
that the proposition of this video

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.800
might be a little bit controversial.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:22.980
The main purpose for me

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.570
why I created this video

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:26.490
is also more to just introduce you

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.290
to this line of thinking,

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.940
and hopefully inspire some of you

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.890
to think more about how
you can turn your career

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:34.770
into a force for good.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.010
This is more important to me

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:37.560
than arguing what exactly

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.850
the most ethical career paths are.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.020
I graduated with a Bachelor of Philosophy

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.040
in my undergrad.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.540
I focused a lot on ethics, metaethics,

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:46.780
and political philosophy.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.080
And here I thought that
this might be a great topic.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.510
It draws from a area of philosophy

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.480
that is called the ethics of career choice

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.570
that investigates exactly these questions.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:57.710
So let's start this video

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.240
with a little thought experiment.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.960
This one got popularized by Peter Singer,

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.970
a quite well-known
contemporary philosopher.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:05.940
He started his career at Oxford,

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.170
and now is a professor at Princeton,

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.680
and he describes the following scenario.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.930
So let's imagine you're
on your way to work,

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.090
and on this way there is a pond,

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.060
And you see in the pond
that there is a kid,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.220
and the kid is screaming for help.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.110
The kid seems to be about to drown.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:24.370
And you are a good swimmer,

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.180
you would be able to just jump in

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.750
and likely pull the kid out of the pond,

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.000
and thereby likely save that kid's life.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:31.970
But would you do it?

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.670
Because if you do it, you
would come late to work.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.550
You're wearing a fancy, tailor-made suit.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.100
The pond looks a bit dirty,

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.450
so likely you will need
to throw away the suit.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.830
This will have cost you
several thousand bucks.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.500
And after all, it's not your kid.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.920
It's not your fault that
the kid is in the pond.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.290
You have nothing to do with it.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.260
So think about this for yourself.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.660
Would you jump in, save the kid,

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.410
even though this leads to damages

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.640
of your clothes of a
couple of thousand dollars?

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.710
And indeed, lots of
research has been conducted

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.550
exactly on this thought experiment,

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:01.830
and I think it's no surprise

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.347
that the big majority of all the people

00:02:03.347 --> 00:02:05.487
confronted with this situation said,

00:02:05.487 --> 00:02:08.100
"Yes, of course, I would save the kid.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.470
Of course, I wouldn't care
if I damaged my clothe."

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.560
Many people would find it outlandish

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.220
that anyone would just
consider not doing that,

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.150
consider not saving the
kid in such a situation.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.980
But if we hold this to be true,

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.230
how is this consistent with the fact

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.980
that every day, people die from hunger,

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.200
thirst, curable illnesses,

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.500
and other consequences of extreme poverty,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:32.560
where it's just the fact

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.200
that the majority of people
don't do anything about.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.850
And here, where it would be possible,

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.550
just with small sums,

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.100
a couple of hundred dollar,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.730
a couple of thousand dollar,

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:44.200
you could save these people.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.780
You could save these people's life.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.280
But this is a contradiction, right,

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.113
that you are facing.

00:02:49.113 --> 00:02:51.440
On the one hand, in this first scenario,

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.400
you would absolutely go for it, do it.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:54.590
On the other scenario,

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.850
most of us apparently do
not seem to be willing

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.760
to save these people's lives,

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.830
even though we would be
able to have an impact.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.050
So now you might ask yourself,

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.320
well, that's interesting and all,

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.320
but what the heck does this have to do

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.660
with ethics of career choice,

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:10.620
with whether a consulting
career is ethical or not?

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:11.920
And so before we dive into this,

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.560
let's pause this for a moment

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.070
and rather agree on some
terms and definitions.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.600
Because, of course, one of
the key questions to answer

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.440
is what do we even mean by that.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.987
What is an ethical
career choice after all?

00:03:22.987 --> 00:03:24.210
And so for this video,

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.060
I'm adopting a consequentialist view,

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.870
which says something like
an ethical career choice

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:30.250
leads to a career path

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.840
that enables you to influence the world

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.160
in a positive way,

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.780
i.e., to have positive
consequences in the world.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:38.990
And surely while, of course,

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:41.150
different types of consequences exist,

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.090
I'd say just to simplify
it a little bit it,

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:45.350
a good proxy would be to save lives,

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.390
or at least significantly improve

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:49.360
the living conditions of other people.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:50.193
And so in my view,

00:03:50.193 --> 00:03:51.970
while surely you might
come up with other aspects

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:53.170
that would inform this,

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:55.210
I'd say it's really difficult to argue

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:59.050
that if a career leads to
positive consequences like this,

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.860
it would not be considered
an ethical career path.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:02.930
Now the next question might be

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.940
how we assess whether a career indeed has

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.930
these positive consequences or not.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.550
And I wanna propose the standard

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.360
of counterfactual reasoning.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:13.510
And what this means is that
to assess the consequences

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:15.630
of your actions in your career path,

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:17.750
you should compare the state of the world

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.630
if you would pursue the career

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:20.850
with the state of the world

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.040
if you would not pursue the career.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.230
And the data of these two situations,

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:27.390
these two states,

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.870
this is the impact of your actions,

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:32.200
the impact of the career that you choose.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.460
Now following these standards,

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.170
what are the best career
paths that you should pursue

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:37.660
to live an ethical life,

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.050
following this definition?

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.420
And here, one fact that I
find to be quite powerful

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.310
is that the average person

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:46.190
works about 80,000 hours

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:48.860
in his or her life, 80,000 hours.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.370
That's a very significant
part of your life indeed,

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.410
so surely we should make sure

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.360
that these 80,000 hours matter.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.640
And there's one quite
popular website in this field

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.110
called 80000hours.org,

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.350
that compares different career paths

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:06.010
exactly by these moral ethical standards.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:07.700
So let's investigate different archetypes

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.840
of career paths for their ethical merits.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.860
So let's start with
the direct benefactors.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:14.080
These are careers that positively impact

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.640
other people in a positive way.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.630
A good example would be a medical doctor.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:18.940
Next are the influencers.

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.670
These are the people that motivate

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.030
and inspire other people to do good,

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.500
people who want to spread the ideas

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:25.760
of effective altruism,

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.270
which is the name of the movement.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.110
And many of the ideas
I'm sharing you today

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:30.110
are drawn from,

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.120
and, of course, these people
can have a great impact

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:33.920
as they multiply the ideas,

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.900
and then many, many other people do good,

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.220
hopefully due to their instigation.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:39.310
Next are researchers.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.710
So people doing research
in relevant fields

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:43.020
that improve the living conditions

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.490
of many people around the world.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:47.420
An example would surely be
research on renewable energies.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:49.140
Last but not least, the high earners.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.180
And these are surely people
working in consulting,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:52.790
working in banking,

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:54.870
in professional services in general.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.930
So people who dedicated their life

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:57.860
to work in careers

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.490
that enables them to earn more

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:01.510
than most other people around them.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:02.770
And then, of course, the ideas

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:05.800
that these people then can use these funds

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:08.080
to donate to organizations

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:09.637
who do improve the world in a better way.

00:06:09.637 --> 00:06:11.240
Though now while, in principle,

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.150
all of these careers can do good,

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:15.240
let's add the additional standard

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.350
of counterfactual reasoning.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:19.097
This one says that to assess your impact,

00:06:19.097 --> 00:06:20.810
you need to envision the world

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.240
if you would not pursue this career

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.130
with the world in which
you do pursue this career.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.660
Let's look into the example
of a medical doctor.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.060
At least in Western countries,

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:33.090
there are many more people
who'd like to become an MD

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:33.923
compared to the people

00:06:33.923 --> 00:06:36.260
actually can study the subject of medicine

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:37.700
and have the chance to do so.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.400
So if you are not pursuing
the career of an MD,

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.470
there will be someone
else filling your spot.

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.360
And your counterfactual contribution,

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:47.870
your counterfactual impact,

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.290
will only be the extent
to which you are better

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:52.920
than the next best candidate on the list

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.800
who would have filled your spot.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:56.700
And, of course, it's
quite difficult to argue

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.250
that this impact is very
significant after all.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.950
So let's contrast that with a career

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:02.690
in banking or in finance.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:05.270
Because here surely the
vast majority of people,

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:06.520
that just a fact,

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.560
do not donate a significant
amount of their salary

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:10.730
every month, every year.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:12.260
So if you are pursuing this career,

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:13.770
potentially are able to replace

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.470
the next best candidate,

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:17.297
who very likely will not have done that,

00:07:17.297 --> 00:07:19.730
and you are a person who
would be willing to donate

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.280
only 10, 20% of your net income

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:23.720
to some charitable causes,

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.810
this data suddenly can
be quite significant,

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:27.800
and you will have a real impact

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:29.950
also by this counterfactual standard.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:31.260
So this is the line of reasoning

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:32.330
that some people employ

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:34.670
to claim that a career
in investment banking

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:36.460
can be more ethical than, for instance,

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:37.630
a career as an MD.

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:39.010
Of course, the caveat to that

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:40.630
is that also as an MD,

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:42.460
at least the many different fields,

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:45.500
you will earn a significant
amount of money as well.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.830
And then, of course,
you have the opportunity

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:49.080
by the fact that you become
a high earner as well

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.870
to upgrade your impact that
you can have on the world

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.690
also by this counterfactual standard.

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:57.190
So let's how the team from
80,000hours.org sees this,

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:58.340
because they really investigate

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:00.510
all types of different career paths.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.270
And their summary, their main conclusion

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.950
is that they identified
these five career paths

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:06.400
that, from their perspective,

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:08.840
are the most ethical, most impactful ones.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:09.673
So what you can see here,

00:08:09.673 --> 00:08:11.230
as well as that becoming an MD

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:12.390
did not really make the list,

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:14.950
but instead, they're talking
about government and policy

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:16.960
in an area irrelevant to a top problem.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.040
This will be one of
their top five choices.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:20.440
Or organization building

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:22.380
and effective nonprofits.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.130
So leading, building up these charities

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:27.110
that really do good in the world.

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:29.000
Third, research in relevant areas.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.100
We just talked about this.

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:32.010
Fourth, applying an unusual skill

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.290
to a needed niche.

00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:34.220
So this is to the point

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:36.050
of you having a very specific,

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:37.600
very special talent

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.930
that you can apply to really
create something good.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:41.660
And then number five, earning to give.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:42.493
So this is, of course,

00:08:42.493 --> 00:08:44.290
exactly what we just talked about.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.670
Pursuing a high-earner career indeed

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:49.590
can be one of the most impactful
things that you can do.

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:50.890
So then indeed they write about,

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:53.060
so what's the best way to earn to give?

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.370
So here, look for some options

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:55.750
where you have a great fit.

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:57.770
Make sure it is a high-earning path

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:58.960
with a good outlook.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:00.740
It offers flexible career capital,

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:02.190
so that you're not locked in,

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:04.270
but can potentially also
transition in other fields.

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.300
And, of course, it doesn't cause harm.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:08.200
So let's talk about this
in a moment as well,

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:09.033
because, of course, I know

00:09:09.033 --> 00:09:10.400
that this is a common prejudice

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:11.800
against consulting or banking,

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:13.570
that they do cause lots of harm indeed.

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:15.000
So what jobs are highest-paying?

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.040
So here they talk about jobs in finance,

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:18.620
management, medicine,

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:20.660
from the perspective of being high-earner,

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.300
law, real estate, and so on.

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:23.720
Then they go on by talking about

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.940
what the best options overall are

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:27.050
to earn to give.

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:28.420
And as you can see here,

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:30.610
management consulting did make the list.

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:32.730
But let's now do briefly
talk about the point

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:34.147
of not causing harm.

00:09:34.147 --> 00:09:35.330
And this is also a question

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:36.880
that they address on their site.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:38.190
And just now to quote them,

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:41.540
they say, but don't many
high-earning jobs cause harm?

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:43.810
Well, of course, don't
recommend taking a job

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:44.930
that does a lot of harm

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:46.880
in order to donate the money.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:49.330
In practice, most people who earn to give

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:50.350
work in the fields of technology

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:53.320
as in management, medicine, or consulting,

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:54.900
and we think that these positions

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.740
do a small amount of good, or are neutral.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:09:59.700
For instance, many financial traders

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.120
make profits at the
expense of other traders,

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:03.780
so they are moving money around,

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:05.887
mostly from rich people
to other rich people.

00:10:05.887 --> 00:10:07.990
And then, of course, there are some people

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:09.640
who cause harm in this industry,

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:11.130
but that's true of any industry.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:12.990
And frankly, this is also my experience,

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:14.760
so I am, of course, also aware

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:16.050
of some of the news reports

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:17.170
of some consulting firms

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:18.430
who allegedly did things,

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:20.590
which really, of course, are outrageous.

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:23.190
And surely if you're a part
of any of these projects,

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:24.470
any of these schemes,

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:26.140
you are definitely doing harm.

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:27.990
While these things apparently do happen,

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:29.940
and, of course, everyone should work hard

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:32.510
in avoiding situations where
these things might arise,

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:33.940
I frankly think it's a bit unfair

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:36.120
to claim that if you work
in professional services

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:36.953
or in consulting,

00:10:36.953 --> 00:10:38.180
you will regularly be exposed

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:39.230
or even an active part

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:41.010
on any of these type of situations.

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:43.053
So assuming you followed
the argument until here,

00:10:43.053 --> 00:10:44.540
you know, did make the choice

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:46.780
to pursue a high-earning career path

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:48.620
with the intention to give back,

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:50.320
to donate some of the money,

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:51.153
well, the question is, well,

00:10:51.153 --> 00:10:52.270
what shall I do with the money?

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:53.970
And here you might be surprised

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:56.030
what impact you already can have

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:57.850
with rather modest sums.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:10:59.220
The different internet sites

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:01.130
comparing organizations and causes

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:02.440
that you could donate to,

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:03.610
one of the, as far as I know,

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:06.690
quite popular websites in
this area is givewell.org.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:08.050
They have the list a couple of causes

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:09.190
which, from their perspective,

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:10.610
have a very high ROI.

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:12.380
Of course, I cannot personally endorse

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:13.970
any of these organizations here.

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:15.680
As always, please do your own research

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:17.140
before you decide to give money

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:18.540
to any of these organizations.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.370
You could donate to malaria medicine.

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:22.000
So apparently for $7,

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.300
you would be able to protect
a child from malaria,

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.060
which would lead to one saved life,

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:29.300
for an average, every
$4,500 that you donate.

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:31.160
Or another example, vitamin supplements.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:34.120
So for on $1, you can
deliver vitamin A supplement,

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:35.277
which, on average, leads to a saved life

00:11:35.277 --> 00:11:38.060
for every $3,000 that you donate.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:39.230
Or here, last on the list,

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:40.510
the deworming treatments.

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:44.040
So for about $1, you are
able to deworm a child,

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:45.920
cure the child from these parasites.

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:48.000
While these parasites might
not be life-threatening,

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:49.720
they significantly decrease

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:51.590
the quality of life of these people,

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:53.620
and often also significantly impact

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:55.910
the earnings potential of
these people in the future

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:56.930
in a negative way.

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:58.430
So these conditions go uncured,

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:00.810
this often means a life in extreme poverty

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.200
for the affected people,

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:03.410
and these people could be helped

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:05.690
for just $1 for deworming treatment.

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:07.110
But I now will, of course, be interested

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:08.500
in your perspective as well.

